\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For arbitrary binary nonnegative row and column margins and binary upper bounds on every cell, is there one randomized relative counting scheme using polynomial worst-case bit time in total input length, $\varepsilon ^{-1}$ and $\log (\delta ^{-1})$, with failure probability $\delta $ and certain zero output for emptiness, without a counting or sampling oracle?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For arbitrary binary nonnegative row and column margins and binary upper bounds on every cell, is there one randomized relative counting scheme using polynomial worst-case bit time in total input length, $\varepsilon ^{-1}$ and $\log (\delta ^{-1})$, with failure probability $\delta $ and certain zero output for emptiness, without a counting or sampling oracle?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://aimath.org/pastworkshops/chipfiringproblems.pdf}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/An-FPRAS-for-Cell-Bounded-Contingency-Tables-September-24-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

